PostgreSQL 19rc2: btree_gist prices a multibyte text key by its bytes past the common prefix, not by a character's.

gbt_var_penalty (btree_utils_var.c) prices adding a key to a subtree, when the key extends the subtree's bounds without
shortening their common prefix, by the bytes just past that prefix. In 19rc2 gbt_var_node_cp_len ends the prefix at a
character boundary in a multibyte encoding, so for UTF8 text the penalty compares the leading bytes of the next
characters, which whole ranges of characters share: all of Latin-1 Supplement begins with C2 or C3, all of the CJK
Unified Ideographs with one of E4 to E9. Adding such a key then costs about the same in every subtree, gistchoose places
it with no regard to its order, the leaf pages' key ranges overlap, and a point lookup descends into many of them: 14
buffers for one row of 20,000 two-ideograph keys, where ASCII keys take 3 (README, PostgreSQL known issues).

This backports master's b82d69abf6 (Tom Lane, 2026-07-03, "Remove btree_gist's useless logic for encoding-aware
truncation", for PostgreSQL 20) byte for byte. It removes the character alignment as dead code, which also fixes the
pricing, and with it gbtree_vinfo.eml, so the gbtree_vinfo structs become const. It adds the regression test text_utf8,
which upstream does not have: it inserts keys of two CJK ideographs into an existing index and uses pageinspect to count
the keys that the ranges of three or more leaf pages cover, most of them with 19rc2's prefix and none with this one, and
checks that lookups by multibyte keys return the right rows. It needs a UTF8 database and is skipped otherwise
(text_utf8_1.out).

b82d69abf6 is on master only, so every PostgreSQL 19 repin carries this until a 19 release includes it.

